window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert11-bio-morphology","kind":"practice","exam":"NEET","year":null,"title":"Morphology of Flowering Plants · Practice","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","subject":"Biology","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-001","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":1,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the androecium is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Each stamen consists of a filament and an anther, and pollen grains are produced in the pollen sacs","In the family Solanaceae, the five stamens are epipetalous","Polyandrous condition refers to stamens united into many bundles","Epipetalous stamens show union of stamens with members of another whorl"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: each stamen has a filament and an anther, and pollen grains form in the pollen sacs of the anther.\n(2) Correct: in Solanaceae the five stamens are epipetalous, i.e. attached to the petals.\n(3) Incorrect: polyandrous means the stamens are free. Stamens united into more than two bundles are polyadelphous, as in citrus.\n(4) Correct: epipetalous means the stamens are united with another whorl, the corolla.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-002","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":2,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the floral characters of Solanaceae is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Calyx has five united, persistent sepals with valvate aestivation","Stamens are five and epipetalous","Ovary is inferior and unilocular with parietal placentation","Flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the calyx has five united sepals. They are persistent and valvate.\n(2) Correct: there are five stamens, and they are epipetalous.\n(3) Incorrect: the Solanaceae ovary is superior, bicarpellary and bilocular. Its placenta is swollen and axile. A unilocular ovary with parietal placentation is the mustard pattern, and the ovary is not inferior either.\n(4) Correct: the flowers are bisexual and actinomorphic.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-003","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":3,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A stamen in a flower has a normal filament but does not produce any pollen grains. What is such a sterile stamen called?","image":"","options":["Staminode","Pistillode","Carpel","Perianth"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls a sterile stamen, one that produces no pollen, a staminode.\nA pistillode is a sterile carpel, not a stamen.\nCarpel and perianth are not parts of the androecium at all.\nSo the sterile stamen described is a staminode.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-004","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":4,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about seeds:\n(a) The hilum is a scar marking the point where the developing seed was attached to the fruit.\n(b) The micropyle is a small pore located just below the hilum.\n(c) In maize, the outer covering of the endosperm is a proteinous aleurone layer.\n(d) The coleoptile is the sheath that encloses the radicle.\n(e) In gram, the endosperm is absent in the mature seed, so reserve food is stored in the fleshy cotyledons.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (c) and (e) only","(a), (b) and (e) only","(c), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c) and (d) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the hilum is the scar where the seed was attached to the fruit.\n(b) Incorrect: the micropyle lies above the hilum, not below it.\n(c) Correct: the aleurone layer is the proteinous outer layer of the maize endosperm.\n(d) Incorrect: the coleoptile encloses the plumule. The coleorhiza encloses the radicle.\n(e) Correct: gram seeds lack endosperm, so food is stored in the fleshy cotyledons.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-005","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":5,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of a carpel is usually present at the tip of the style and serves as the receptive surface for pollen grains?","image":"","options":["Ovary","Placenta","Ovule","Stigma"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: A carpel has three parts: the ovary at the base, the style in the middle and the stigma at the tip.\nThe stigma is at the tip of the style. It is the receptive surface that receives pollen grains, so (4) fits.\nThe ovary is the enlarged basal part. It bears ovules on a cushion-like placenta. Ovules and placenta lie inside the ovary, not at the tip of the style.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-006","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":6,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower is described as bracteate when it has:","image":"","options":["Sepals fused into a tube","A reduced leaf at the base of its pedicel","Floral parts in multiples of three","A thalamus that encloses the ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A bract is a reduced leaf found at the base of the pedicel.\nFlowers that have bracts are called bracteate. Flowers without them are ebracteate.\nThe other options describe different terms. Fused sepals form a gamosepalous calyx. Parts in multiples of three make a flower trimerous. A thalamus enclosing the ovary marks an epigynous condition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-007","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":7,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: An anther is usually bilobed, and each lobe has two chambers called pollen-sacs.\nStatement II: In a stamen, pollen grains are produced inside the filament.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A typical anther is bilobed and each lobe has two pollen-sacs, giving four pollen-sacs in all.\nStatement II is incorrect. Pollen grains are produced in the pollen-sacs of the anther. The filament is only the stalk that attaches the anther to the flower.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-008","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":8,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Condition of stamens)\nA. Monoadelphous\nB. Diadelphous\nC. Polyadelphous\nD. Epiphyllous\nList-II (Example)\nI. Pea\nII. Lily\nIII. China rose\nIV. Citrus\nChoose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Monoadelphous means stamens in one bundle, as in china rose (III).\nB. Diadelphous means stamens in two bundles, as in pea (I).\nC. Polyadelphous means stamens in more than two bundles, as in citrus (IV).\nD. Epiphyllous means stamens attached to the perianth, as in lily (II).\nThe common trap is swapping pea and china rose, which is what option (2) does.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-009","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":9,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the wheat plant, the root system consists of a large number of roots arising from the base of the stem.\nReason (R): In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: wheat has a fibrous root system, with many roots arising from the base of the stem.\nR is true: in monocots such as wheat, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by a large number of roots.\nR explains A: these replacing roots are exactly the many roots from the stem base, so they form the fibrous system.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-010","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":10,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The androecium of mustard is written as $A_{2+4}$ in its floral formula, and NCERT cites mustard as an example of variation in filament length. Which interpretation is correct?","image":"","options":["Six stamens united into two bundles, i.e., diadelphous","Six free stamens in two groups, with filaments not all of the same length","Six stamens that are all attached to the petals","Two fertile stamens and four staminodes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a floral formula, '+' separates groups of parts. It does not indicate fusion.\nFusion (cohesion) would be shown by brackets, for example $A_{(9)+1}$ in pea. Adhesion to other parts would be shown by a connecting line.\nSo $A_{2+4}$ means six free stamens in two groups: 2 outer short and 4 inner long (tetradynamous). This matches the unequal filament lengths.\nOption (1) is the trap: reading '2+4' as two bundles is wrong because there are no brackets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-011","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":11,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A banana plant produces fruits even though its ovaries were never fertilised. What is such a fruit called?","image":"","options":["Drupe","Parthenocarpic fruit","Endospermic fruit","Apocarpous fruit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: A fruit that develops without fertilisation of the ovary is a parthenocarpic fruit. Banana is the standard NCERT example, so (2) fits.\nWhy not drupe: A drupe is named for its pericarp structure, such as the stony endocarp of mango. The term says nothing about whether fertilisation took place.\n'Endospermic' describes seeds, not fruits. 'Apocarpous' describes free carpels in a gynoecium.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-012","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":12,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which region of the root tip are root hairs formed?","image":"","options":["Region of meristematic activity","Root cap","Region of elongation","Region of maturation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: cells just above (proximal to) the elongation zone differentiate and mature. This is the region of maturation.\nSome epidermal cells here grow into fine, thread-like root hairs.\nWhy the others fail: the meristematic and elongation regions are busy dividing and lengthening, so no root hairs form there. The root cap only protects the tip.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-013","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":13,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral formula of a flowering plant, what does the symbol P represent?","image":"","options":["Pedicel","Perianth","Petals","Placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a floral formula, P stands for perianth. It is used when the calyx and corolla cannot be told apart, as in lily (family Liliaceae).\nPetals are not written as P. They appear under C (corolla), and sepals appear under K (calyx).\nSo 'Petals' is the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-014","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":14,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower whose sepals are united with one another is described as having a calyx that is","image":"","options":["Polysepalous","Gamosepalous","Gamopetalous","Polypetalous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: 'Gamo-' means united and 'poly-' means free. The suffix '-sepalous' refers to the calyx, and '-petalous' refers to the corolla.\nA calyx with united sepals is therefore gamosepalous.\nPolysepalous describes free sepals.\nGamopetalous is the tempting trap. It also means 'united', but it describes petals, not sepals.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-015","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":15,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): In a flower, the shoot apical meristem changes into a floral meristem, the internodes do not elongate and the axis becomes condensed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT treats the flower as a modified shoot.\nR is true: the shoot apical meristem changes into a floral meristem. The internodes do not elongate, so the axis becomes condensed.\nFloral appendages then arise at successive nodes in place of leaves.\nThis transformation is exactly why the flower counts as a modified shoot, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-016","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":16,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (feature).\nList-I:\nA. Marginal\nB. Axile\nC. Parietal\nD. Basal\nList-II:\nI. Ovules on the inner wall; a false septum makes the ovary two-chambered\nII. A single ovule attached to a placenta at the base of the ovary\nIII. Ovules on a ridge along the ventral suture in two rows\nIV. Ovules attached to an axial placenta in a multilocular ovary","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Marginal: the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture and bears ovules in two rows, as in pea. So A-III.\nB. Axile: ovules sit on a central axial placenta in a multilocular ovary, as in china rose. So B-IV.\nC. Parietal: ovules are on the inner ovary wall, and a false septum makes the ovary two-chambered, as in mustard. So C-I.\nD. Basal: a single ovule is attached at the base of the ovary, as in sunflower. So D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-017","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":17,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I (Condition of stamens)\nA. Monoadelphous\nB. Diadelphous\nC. Polyadelphous\nD. Epipetalous\nList-II (Example)\nI. Citrus\nII. Brinjal\nIII. China rose\nIV. Pea\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Monoadelphous (one bundle) matches china rose (III).\nB. Diadelphous (two bundles) matches pea (IV).\nC. Polyadelphous (many bundles) matches citrus (I).\nD. Epipetalous (attached to petals) matches brinjal (II).\nThis gives A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II. Do not swap china rose and pea, which is the common slip in option (2).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-018","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":18,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The term aestivation refers to","image":"","options":["the arrangement of ovules within the ovary","the mode of arrangement of sepals or petals in the floral bud with respect to other members of the same whorl","the arrangement of flowers on the floral axis","the pattern of arrangement of leaves on the stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): Aestivation is the way sepals or petals are arranged in a floral bud relative to the other members of the same whorl.\nOption (2) matches this definition exactly.\nThe other options describe different terms. Ovule arrangement in the ovary is placentation. Flower arrangement on the axis is inflorescence. Leaf arrangement on the stem is phyllotaxy.\nThe most tempting wrong option is (1), because placentation is also a floral term. It refers to ovules, not to perianth parts.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-019","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":19,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student describes a fleshy fruit and its seed with the following statements:\n(i) The fruit is a ripened ovary, so its wall (pericarp) comes from the ovary wall.\n(ii) The hard stone around the seed of mango is the endocarp.\n(iii) The coir (fibre) of coconut is the endocarp.\n(iv) The seed inside develops from the fertilised ovule.\nWhich statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(i), (ii) and (iv) only","(i), (iii) and (iv) only","(ii) and (iii) only","(i), (ii), (iii) and (iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(i) Correct. A fruit is a ripened ovary, so its pericarp comes from the ovary wall.\n(ii) Correct. The stony layer around the mango seed is the endocarp.\n(iii) Incorrect. Coconut coir is the fibrous mesocarp, not the endocarp. The endocarp is the hard shell.\n(iv) Correct. A seed develops from a fertilised ovule.\nSo statements (i), (ii) and (iv) are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-020","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":20,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the hilum of a dicotyledonous seed?","image":"","options":["A small pore through which water enters the seed","The inner layer of the seed coat","A scar on the seed coat where the developing seed was attached to the fruit","The point where the radicle joins the cotyledons"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: The hilum is a scar on the seed coat. It marks where the developing seed was attached to the fruit, so (3) fits.\nWhy not (1): The small pore just above the hilum is the micropyle.\nThe inner layer of the seed coat is the tegmen, and the outer layer is the testa.\nThe radicle and cotyledons are parts of the embryo axis, not the hilum.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-021","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":21,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Endosperm, a product of double fertilization in angiosperms is absent in the seeds of","image":"","options":["Coconut","Orchids","Maize","Castor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Coconut, maize and castor have albuminous (endospermic) seeds. Coconut water and coconut meat are endosperm. Maize grains and castor seeds also retain endosperm.\nOrchid seeds are minute, dust-like seeds. They lack a functional endosperm at maturity and depend on fungal (mycorrhizal) association to germinate.\nSo the seed without endosperm is that of orchids.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q6)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-022","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":22,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\nA. Marginal\nB. Axile\nC. Parietal\nD. Free central\nList-II:\nI. Dianthus\nII. Argemone\nIII. Pea\nIV. Lemon","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A. Marginal: the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture of a one-chambered ovary, as in pea → III.\nB. Axile: the placenta is axial in a multilocular ovary, as in lemon (also china rose and tomato) → IV.\nC. Parietal: ovules lie on the inner ovary wall, and a false septum makes the ovary two-chambered, as in Argemone and mustard → II.\nD. Free central: ovules sit on a central axis and septa are absent, as in Dianthus and Primrose → I.\nThe matching is A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I. Do not swap the parietal and free central examples.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-023","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":23,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A fruit that develops from the ovary without fertilisation is called a:","image":"","options":["Drupe","Parthenocarpic fruit","Berry","Capsule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A fruit is normally a mature, ripened ovary that develops after fertilisation.\nWhen the ovary develops into a fruit without fertilisation, the fruit is called parthenocarpic. Such fruits are usually seedless.\nDrupe, berry and capsule are fruit types that normally form after fertilisation, so they do not fit the description.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-024","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":24,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a longitudinal view of a root tip, a zone lies a few millimetres above the root cap. Its cells are very small, thin-walled and have dense protoplasm, and they divide repeatedly. This zone is the:","image":"","options":["Region of elongation","Region of maturation","Region of meristematic activity","Root hair zone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The region of meristematic activity lies a few millimetres above the root cap.\nIts cells are very small, thin-walled and densely cytoplasmic, and they divide repeatedly. This matches the description.\nThe region of elongation lies just above it. There, cells enlarge and lengthen rather than divide.\nThe region of maturation, which bears the root hairs, lies further back.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-025","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":25,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral bud of china rose, one margin of each petal overlaps the next petal, and this continues around the whorl. This type of aestivation is","image":"","options":["Valvate","Imbricate","Twisted","Vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: In twisted aestivation, one margin of each petal overlaps the next one, and the pattern repeats regularly around the whorl.\nChina rose, lady's finger and cotton are the NCERT examples of twisted aestivation.\nImbricate (option 2) is the tempting wrong choice. There the margins also overlap, but not in any particular direction.\nValvate means the margins only touch without overlapping. Vexillary applies to the pea-type corolla.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-026","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":26,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived.\nStatement II: In a tap root system, the secondary and tertiary roots arise from the base of the stem.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In monocots, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots from the stem base.\nStatement II is incorrect. In a tap root system, secondary and tertiary roots are lateral branches of the primary root.\nRoots arising from the stem base are the feature of the fibrous root system, not the tap root system.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-027","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":27,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: In the banyan tree and Monstera, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots.\nStatement II: In mustard, the primary root formed by direct elongation of the radicle bears lateral roots of several orders, and together they constitute the tap root system.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer.","image":"","options":["Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: roots arising from parts other than the radicle are adventitious. Grass, Monstera and the banyan tree are examples.\nStatement II is correct: in mustard, the radicle elongates directly into the primary root. This bears secondary, tertiary and further lateral roots, and together they form the tap root system.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-028","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":28,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Valvate\nB. Twisted\nC. Imbricate\nD. Vexillary\nList-II\nI. Gulmohur\nII. Bean\nIII. Calotropis\nIV. Lady's finger\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Valvate: the margins only touch without overlapping, as in Calotropis, so A-III.\nB. Twisted: each margin overlaps the next in one direction, as in lady's finger, so B-IV.\nC. Imbricate: the margins overlap in no particular direction, as in gulmohur, so C-I.\nD. Vexillary: a large standard petal overlaps the wings, which overlap the keel, as in bean, so D-II.\nThe common trap is putting gulmohur under vexillary.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-029","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":29,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of a carpel acts as the receptive surface for pollen grains?","image":"","options":["Ovary","Style","Stigma","Placenta"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A carpel has three parts: stigma, style and ovary.\nThe stigma, usually at the tip of the style, is the receptive surface that catches pollen grains.\nThe style is only the tube joining the stigma to the ovary.\nThe placenta is the tissue inside the ovary that bears the ovules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-030","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":30,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In wheat plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by a large number of roots. From which part of the plant do these replacing roots arise?","image":"","options":["Base of the stem","Tip of the radicle","Secondary roots of the primary root","Axils of the leaves"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: in monocots like wheat, the primary root is short-lived. A large number of roots then arise from the base of the stem, forming the fibrous root system.\nWhy the others fail: roots from the radicle and its secondary branches make up a tap root system, not a fibrous one. Leaf axils bear buds, not these roots.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-031","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":31,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements regarding root systems is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In mustard, the primary root and its branches together constitute the tap root system","In wheat, the roots of the fibrous system originate from the base of the stem","In Monstera, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle","Synthesis of plant growth regulators is carried out by the shoot system only and not by roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in mustard, the primary root and its lateral branches make up the tap root system.\n(2) Correct: wheat's fibrous roots arise from the base of the stem.\n(3) Correct: Monstera has adventitious roots, which arise from parts other than the radicle.\n(4) Incorrect: NCERT lists synthesis of plant growth regulators as a main function of roots. So it is not done by the shoot alone.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-032","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":32,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["In epigynous flowers, the margin of the thalamus grows upward, encloses the ovary and fuses with it, so the other floral parts arise above the ovary","A flower bearing only carpels is unisexual","Plum and guava both have a half-inferior ovary because their floral parts arise on the rim of the thalamus","When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, that flower is always solitary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. In epigynous flowers the thalamus margin grows up, encloses and fuses with the ovary, so the other parts arise above it.\n(2) Correct. A flower with only carpels, i.e. only the gynoecium, is unisexual.\n(3) Incorrect. Plum is perigynous with a half-inferior ovary, but guava is epigynous with an inferior ovary.\n(4) Correct. NCERT states that when a shoot tip transforms into a flower, it is always solitary.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-033","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":33,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A typical anther is bilobed and each lobe has two chambers. How many pollen sacs are present in such an anther?","image":"","options":["Two","Four","One","Eight"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the anther has 2 lobes, and each lobe has 2 chambers.\nEach chamber is a pollen sac, so the number of pollen sacs = $2 \\times 2 = 4$.\nPollen grains form inside these sacs, which is why a typical anther is called tetrasporangiate.\nTwo is the number of lobes, not the number of sacs, so option (1) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-034","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":34,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the embryo of a maize grain, the radicle is enclosed in a protective sheath. What is this sheath called?","image":"","options":["Coleoptile","Scutellum","Coleorhiza","Aleurone layer"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: In the maize embryo, the radicle and root cap are enclosed in a sheath called the coleorhiza, so (3) fits.\nThe plumule is enclosed in a different sheath, the coleoptile. Students often swap these two terms.\nThe scutellum is the single shield-shaped cotyledon. The aleurone layer is the proteinaceous outer layer of the endosperm.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-035","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":35,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"What is the arrangement of ovules within the ovary called?","image":"","options":["Aestivation","Placentation","Phyllotaxy","Venation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Placentation is the arrangement of ovules within the ovary.\nAestivation is the arrangement of sepals or petals in the flower bud.\nPhyllotaxy is the arrangement of leaves on the stem.\nVenation is the arrangement of veins in the leaf lamina.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-036","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":36,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The region of the stem at which leaves are borne is called the:","image":"","options":["Internode","Node","Axil","Rachis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): the stem has nodes and internodes, and leaves are borne at the nodes.\nThe internode is the portion of stem between two successive nodes, so it is the most tempting wrong option. It bears no leaves.\nThe axil is the upper angle between the leaf and the stem. The rachis is the axis of a pinnately compound leaf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-037","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":37,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the family Solanaceae:\n(a) The calyx is persistent and shows valvate aestivation.\n(b) The five stamens are epiphyllous.\n(c) The ovary is superior and bilocular, with axile placentation.\n(d) Leaves are alternate, usually exstipulate, with parallel venation.\n(e) The fruit is a berry or capsule, and the seeds are many and endospermous.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (c) only","(a), (c) and (e) only","(b), (d) and (e) only","(a), (c), (d) and (e) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: the calyx has five united, persistent sepals with valvate aestivation.\n(b) Incorrect: the five stamens are epipetalous, not epiphyllous.\n(c) Correct: the ovary is bicarpellary, syncarpous, superior and bilocular, with a swollen placenta and axile placentation.\n(d) Incorrect: the leaves are alternate and simple, but the venation is reticulate, not parallel.\n(e) Correct: the fruit is a berry or capsule, and the seeds are many and endospermous.\nThe correct statements are (a), (c) and (e).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-038","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":38,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which structure covers the apex of a root like a thimble and protects the tender tip as it moves through the soil?","image":"","options":["Root hair","Root cap","Region of maturation","Coleorhiza"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the root apex is covered by a thimble-like root cap. It protects the tender tip as the root pushes through the soil.\nWhy the others fail: root hairs are absorptive outgrowths found in the maturation zone. The coleorhiza is a sheath around the radicle in monocot seeds, not part of the growing root tip.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-039","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":39,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the leaflets are borne on a common axis called the rachis. What does the rachis represent?","image":"","options":["The petiole of the leaf","The midrib of the leaf","The leaf base","A modified axillary branch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a pinnately compound leaf, the incisions of the lamina reach the midrib and split the blade into many leaflets.\nThe leaflets stay attached to this common axis, the rachis. So the rachis represents the midrib of the leaf.\nIt is not the petiole, which is the stalk below the first leaflets. In palmately compound leaves, the leaflets arise together at the tip of the petiole.\nThe leaf base and axillary branches play no part in forming the rachis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-040","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":40,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds a green, flattened structure with a midrib and veins attached to a plant axis. Which observation would best confirm that it is a complete leaf and not a leaflet of a compound leaf?","image":"","options":["It has a prominent midrib with veins","It contains chlorophyll and photosynthesises","Its veinlets form a network","A bud is present in its axil"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a leaf arises at a node and bears a bud in its axil. The leaflets of a compound leaf have no axillary buds.\nSo finding an axillary bud confirms the structure is a complete leaf.\nA midrib with veins, chlorophyll and reticulate veinlets are tempting choices. But leaflets of compound leaves have all of these too, so they cannot tell a leaf from a leaflet.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-041","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":41,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of a hypogynous flower is described as superior.\nReason (R): In a hypogynous flower, the gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus and the other floral parts are situated below it.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in a hypogynous flower the ovary is called superior.\nR is true: the gynoecium sits at the highest point of the thalamus. The sepals, petals and stamens arise below it. Examples are mustard, china rose and brinjal.\nThe ovary is called 'superior' because of this position above the other whorls, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-042","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":42,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A plant has leaves whose bases form a sheath around the stem and whose veins run parallel to each other within the lamina. Which other feature is this plant most likely to show?","image":"","options":["A persistent tap root developing from the radicle","A fibrous root system arising from the base of the stem","Reticulate venation in its younger leaves","Two cotyledons in its seed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A sheathing leaf base and parallel venation are both monocot features.\nIn monocots, the primary root is short-lived. It is replaced by a fibrous root system arising from the base of the stem.\nThe other options are dicot features:\n- a persistent tap root growing from the radicle\n- reticulate venation\n- two cotyledons in the seed\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-043","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":43,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the family Solanaceae are correct?\n(i) Leaves are exstipulate with reticulate venation\n(ii) Calyx is persistent with valvate aestivation\n(iii) Stamens are five and epipetalous\n(iv) Ovary is inferior and unilocular with parietal placentation\n(v) Gynoecium is bicarpellary, obliquely placed and syncarpous\n(vi) Seeds are few and non-endospermous","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: leaves are exstipulate with reticulate venation.\n(ii) Correct: the calyx is united, persistent and valvate.\n(iii) Correct: there are five epipetalous stamens.\n(iv) Incorrect: the ovary is superior and bilocular, with axile placentation on a swollen placenta.\n(v) Correct: the gynoecium is bicarpellary, obliquely placed and syncarpous.\n(vi) Incorrect: seeds are many and endospermous.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-044","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":44,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The lamina of a leaf is deeply incised, but none of the incisions reaches the midrib. This leaf should be classified as:","image":"","options":["Pinnately compound, because the lamina is divided","Palmately compound, because the lobes radiate from one point","Simple, because the incisions do not touch the midrib","Compound, because the lamina is not entire"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: a leaf is simple when its lamina is entire, or when its incisions do not touch the midrib.\nA leaf becomes compound only when the incisions reach the midrib and break the lamina into separate leaflets.\nHere the incisions stop short of the midrib, so the leaf is simple however deep its lobes are.\nOptions (1), (2) and (4) wrongly treat deep lobing alone as compoundness.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-045","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":45,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of phyllotaxy) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Alternate\nB. Opposite\nC. Whorled\nList-II\n(i) Alstonia\n(ii) Guava\n(iii) Mustard\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(ii), C-(i)","A-(ii), B-(iii), C-(i)","A-(i), B-(ii), C-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Alternate means one leaf per node. Mustard is the example, so A–(iii).\nB. Opposite means a pair of leaves per node, facing each other. Guava is the example, so B–(ii).\nC. Whorled means more than two leaves per node. Alstonia is the example, so C–(i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-046","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":46,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The corolla of pea shows vexillary aestivation.\nReason (R): In vexillary aestivation, the keel petals overlap the wings, which in turn overlap the standard.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. Pea has a papilionaceous corolla with vexillary aestivation.\nR is false. In vexillary aestivation the large standard overlaps the two wings, and the wings overlap the two keel petals.\nR states this overlap in reverse order, so it cannot be accepted.\nSince A is true and R is false, option (3) is correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-047","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":47,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The mature seeds of plants such as gram and peas, possess no endosperm, because","image":"","options":["These plants are not angiosperms","There is no double fertilization in them","Endosperm is not formed in them","Endosperm gets used up by the developing embryo during seed development"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Gram and pea are angiosperms (dicots of Fabaceae). They show double fertilization, so a triploid endosperm is formed.\nDuring seed development, the growing embryo absorbs this endosperm completely. The food is stored in the thick cotyledons instead.\nSo the mature seed is non-endospermic (exalbuminous).\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) are wrong: these plants are angiosperms, they do have double fertilization, and endosperm does form initially.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q3)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-048","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":48,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): In a flower, the internodes elongate greatly so that the floral whorls are widely separated on the axis.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true: a flower is a modified shoot. The shoot apical meristem changes into a floral meristem, and the floral appendages are borne at successive nodes.\nR is false: in a flower the internodes do not elongate. The axis becomes condensed, so the floral whorls (calyx, corolla, androecium, gynoecium) lie close together on the thalamus.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-049","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":49,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The stem of many flowering plants is green when young.\nReason (R): The stem of many flowering plants later becomes woody and dark brown.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: the stem is generally green when young.\nR is true: the stem later often becomes woody and dark brown.\nR describes a later change in the stem. It does not explain why young stems are green, so it is not the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-050","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":50,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following plants are the stamens united into two bundles (diadelphous condition)?","image":"","options":["China rose","Citrus","Brinjal","Pea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Diadelphous means the stamens are united into two bundles. Pea is the NCERT example.\nChina rose is monoadelphous, with stamens in one bundle.\nCitrus is polyadelphous, with stamens in many bundles.\nBrinjal is epipetalous, with stamens attached to the petals. This is not fusion into bundles.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-051","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":51,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a racemose inflorescence, the main axis continues to grow. In what order are the flowers borne on it?","image":"","options":["Basipetal succession, with the oldest flower at the top","Acropetal succession, with the oldest flowers at the base","A single terminal flower only","Random order with no age gradient"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a racemose inflorescence the main axis keeps growing. It does not end in a flower.\nNew flowers form laterally near the growing apex. So the older flowers stay towards the base and the younger ones lie near the tip. This is acropetal succession.\nOption (1) is tempting, but basipetal order, with the oldest flower at the top, belongs to cymose inflorescence. A single terminal flower is the solitary condition.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-052","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":52,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A leaf whose lamina is deeply incised is always a compound leaf.\nStatement II: A leaf is compound only when the incisions of the lamina reach up to the midrib, breaking it into leaflets.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. A lamina may be deeply incised and still be simple, as long as the incisions do not reach the midrib.\nStatement II is correct. A leaf is compound only when the incisions reach the midrib and break the lamina into separate leaflets.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-053","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":53,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Many pulses of daily use belong to one of the families below (tick the correct answer)","image":"","options":["Solanaceae","Fabaceae","Liliaceae","Poaceae"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pulses are legumes, so their seeds develop inside a pod (legume fruit).\nGram, arhar, moong, urad, pea and soybean all belong to Fabaceae (Papilionoideae).\nThe other families are wrong:\n- Solanaceae gives potato, tomato and brinjal.\n- Liliaceae includes onion-like and ornamental plants.\n- Poaceae gives cereals such as wheat, rice and maize, not pulses.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q7)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-054","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":54,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The growth of a root in length is mainly due to the cells of which region?","image":"","options":["Root cap","Region of elongation","Region of maturation","Root hair zone"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: cells proximal to the meristematic zone undergo rapid elongation and enlargement. This is what increases the root's length.\nTempting wrong idea: the meristem adds new cells, but the actual lengthening happens in the region of elongation.\nWhy the others fail: the root cap only protects the tip. The maturation (root hair) zone differentiates cells rather than lengthening the root.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-055","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":55,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student finds a green, leaf-like structure on a twig and must decide whether it is a single leaf or a leaflet of a compound leaf. Which observation would best confirm that it is a leaflet?","image":"","options":["It has a prominent midrib with reticulate venation","No bud is present in its axil, while a bud is present in the axil of the petiole of the common axis bearing it","It is borne at a node of the stem in an alternate arrangement","Its lamina shows incisions that do not reach the midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: an axillary bud occurs in the axil of the petiole of simple and compound leaves, but never in the axil of a leaflet.\nOption (2) fits because a bud is absent at the leaflet but present at the base of the common petiole. This confirms the structure is a leaflet.\nOption (1) fails: simple leaves also have a midrib and reticulate venation.\nOption (3) fails: being borne at a stem node suggests a whole leaf, not a leaflet.\nOption (4) fails: incisions that do not reach the midrib indicate a simple leaf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-056","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":56,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane passing through its centre. Such a flower is described as asymmetric. Which of the following is an example?","image":"","options":["Datura","Canna","Cassia","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An asymmetric (irregular) flower cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane through its centre.\nCanna is the standard NCERT example, so it fits.\nDatura and chilli are actinomorphic: any radial plane gives equal halves.\nCassia is the tempting wrong option. It is zygomorphic, not asymmetric, because one vertical plane does give two similar halves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-057","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":57,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I:\n(a) China rose\n(b) Calotropis\n(c) Alstonia\n(d) Neem\nList-II:\n(i) Whorled phyllotaxy\n(ii) Pinnately compound leaf\n(iii) Alternate phyllotaxy\n(iv) Opposite phyllotaxy\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) China rose has one leaf per node, so it shows alternate phyllotaxy: (iii).\n(b) Calotropis has a pair of opposite leaves per node, so it shows opposite phyllotaxy: (iv).\n(c) Alstonia has more than two leaves per node, so it shows whorled phyllotaxy: (i).\n(d) Neem has a pinnately compound leaf: (ii).\nOnly option (1) gives all four pairs correctly.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-058","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":58,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Venation is a term used to describe the pattern of arrangement of","image":"","options":["Floral organs","Flower in inflorescence","Veins and veinlets in a lamina","All of them"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Venation is the arrangement of veins and veinlets in the lamina (leaf blade).\nIt is of two types:\n- reticulate, a net-like pattern typical of dicots;\n- parallel, typical of monocots.\nThe arrangement of floral organs is described by terms like aestivation. The arrangement of flowers on an axis is called inflorescence. So 'All of them' is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q5)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-059","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":59,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the majority of dicotyledonous plants, the primary root is formed by the direct elongation of which part of the embryo?","image":"","options":["Plumule","Cotyledon","Radicle","Base of the stem"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in most dicots, the primary root forms by direct elongation of the radicle.\nThe radicle is the embryonic root, so this fits option (3).\nThe tempting wrong option is the plumule. It is the embryonic shoot and develops into the stem, not the root.\nCotyledons are seed leaves that store or absorb food.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-060","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":60,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of mustard appears two-chambered.\nReason (R): In parietal placentation, a one-chambered ovary becomes two-chambered due to the formation of a false septum.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Mustard (Brassicaceae) has a syncarpous ovary that looks two-chambered in transverse section.\nR is true. In parietal placentation the ovary is basically one-chambered. It becomes two-chambered when a false septum (replum) forms.\nR explains A. Mustard has parietal placentation, and its false septum is exactly why the ovary appears two-chambered.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-061","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":61,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about placentation is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In parietal placentation, the ovary is one-chambered but becomes two-chambered due to a false septum, as in mustard","In free central placentation, ovules are borne on a central axis and septa are absent, as in Dianthus","In basal placentation, the placenta develops at the base of the ovary and bears a single ovule, as in marigold","In marginal placentation, the placenta forms a ridge along the dorsal suture of the ovary, as in pea"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: mustard has a one-chambered ovary made two-chambered by a false septum.\n(2) Correct: in Dianthus the ovules sit on a central axis and there are no septa.\n(3) Correct: in marigold a basal placenta bears a single ovule.\n(4) Incorrect: in pea the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture, not the dorsal suture. The ovules are borne in two rows on this ridge.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-062","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":62,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following flowers are the carpels free from one another (apocarpous gynoecium)?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Tomato","China rose","Lotus"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"An apocarpous gynoecium has free carpels. Lotus and rose are the NCERT examples.\nMustard, tomato and china rose all have fused carpels, so their gynoecium is syncarpous.\nTherefore lotus is the only apocarpous flower among the options.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-063","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":63,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have non-endospermous (non-endospermic) seeds?\nCastor, bean, gram, pea, maize, orchid, wheat","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Non-endospermous seeds: bean, gram and pea, where food is stored in the cotyledons. Orchid is also non-endospermous, an exception among monocots.\nEndospermous seeds: castor (a dicot with endosperm), maize and wheat (cereals with bulky endosperm).\nCount of non-endospermous seeds = 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-064","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":64,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show imbricate aestivation?\nCassia, gulmohur, Calotropis, cotton, lady's finger, china rose","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Cassia: imbricate, so it counts.\nGulmohur: imbricate, so it counts.\nCalotropis: valvate, so it does not count.\nCotton, lady's finger and china rose: twisted, so they do not count.\nOnly two plants, Cassia and gulmohur, show imbricate aestivation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-065","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":65,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a racemose inflorescence, which of the following correctly describes the main axis and the order in which flowers are borne?","image":"","options":["The main axis continues to grow and flowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession","The main axis ends in a flower and flowers are borne in basipetal order","The main axis continues to grow and flowers are borne in basipetal order","The main axis ends in a flower and flowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a racemose inflorescence the main axis keeps growing. It does not end in a flower.\nFlowers are borne laterally in acropetal succession. Older flowers lie at the base and younger ones near the apex.\nOptions (2) and (4) describe an axis that ends in a flower, which is cymose.\nOption (3) pairs continued growth with basipetal order, which does not happen. Basipetal order belongs to cymose inflorescences.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-066","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":66,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The potato (Solanum tuberosum) grows underground, yet it is considered a stem and not a root. Which reason best justifies this?","image":"","options":["It stores reserve food material","It grows inside the soil away from light","It bears nodes with buds that can grow into new shoots","It develops directly from the radicle of the embryo"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: nodes and internodes with buds are the defining features of a stem. Roots never bear them.\nThe 'eyes' of a potato are buds at nodes that grow into new shoots, so the potato is a modified underground stem.\n(1) fails because roots such as carrot also store food, so storage cannot decide the matter.\n(2) and (4) describe root traits, not stem traits.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-067","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":67,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Petals of most flowers are brightly coloured.\nReason (R): Bright petals attract insects that bring about pollination.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT notes that the corolla is usually brightly coloured.\nR is true: bright colours attract insects, which carry out pollination.\nR gives the purpose of the bright colour, so it correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-068","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":68,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In a cymose inflorescence the main axis ends in a flower, so the oldest flower lies at the tip of the axis","When a shoot tip transforms into a flower, the flower is always solitary","The ray florets of sunflower have a superior ovary because the other floral parts arise below the ovary","In a racemose inflorescence the youngest flowers are found nearest the apex of the main axis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: in a cymose inflorescence the main axis ends in a flower, so the oldest flower is at the tip.\n(2) Correct: NCERT states that a shoot tip transformed into a flower is always solitary.\n(3) Incorrect: ray florets of sunflower are epigynous. Their ovary is inferior, and the other parts arise above it, not below.\n(4) Correct: racemose flowers form acropetally, so the youngest flowers lie near the apex.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-069","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":69,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a floral diagram, a dot is drawn at the top. What does this dot indicate?","image":"","options":["Position of the bract","Position of the mother axis with respect to the flower","Position of the stigma","Number of carpels in the ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a floral diagram, the dot drawn at the top shows where the mother axis lies relative to the flower.\nThe whorls are then drawn in circles around the centre: calyx outermost, then corolla, then androecium, with the gynoecium at the centre.\nThe bract is not shown as a dot at the top; it is drawn below the flower.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-070","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":70,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Long, thin, flexible petioles help keep the leaf cool.\nReason (R): Such petioles allow the leaf blade to flutter in the wind, which also brings fresh air to the leaf surface.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT states that long, thin, flexible petioles help cool the leaf.\nR is true. Such petioles let the leaf blade flutter in the wind, which brings fresh air to the leaf surface.\nThe fluttering in R is the mechanism that cools the leaf. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-071","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":71,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(a) Node\n(b) Internode\n(c) Plumule\n(d) Axillary bud\nList-II\n(i) Embryonic part that gives rise to the stem\n(ii) Portion of stem between two leaf-bearing points\n(iii) Develops into a branch\n(iv) Point on stem where a leaf is borne","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Node – (iv): the point on the stem where a leaf is borne.\n(b) Internode – (ii): the portion of stem between two nodes.\n(c) Plumule – (i): the embryonic part that develops into the stem.\n(d) Axillary bud – (iii): grows into a branch.\nThe common trap is swapping node and internode, as option (2) does.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-072","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":72,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following flowers have a superior ovary?\nMustard, China rose, Brinjal, Tomato, Plum, Guava, Peach, Cucumber","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Hypogynous flowers have the other floral parts arising below the ovary, so the ovary is superior. Mustard, china rose, brinjal and tomato are hypogynous, which makes 4.\nPerigynous flowers (plum, peach) have a half-inferior ovary, so they are not counted.\nEpigynous flowers (guava, cucumber) have an inferior ovary fused with the thalamus.\nThe count of flowers with a superior ovary is therefore 4.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-073","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":73,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the floral bud of Calotropis, the petals of the corolla only touch one another at their margins and do not overlap. This type of aestivation is called:","image":"","options":["Twisted","Imbricate","Valvate","Vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Aestivation is the arrangement of sepals or petals in a bud relative to other members of the same whorl.\nWhen the margins only touch edge to edge without overlapping, it is valvate, as in Calotropis.\nTwisted, imbricate and vexillary aestivation all involve some overlapping of margins, so they do not fit.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-074","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":74,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Rose and lotus possess an apocarpous gynoecium.\nStatement II: In free central placentation, the ovules are borne on a central axis and septa are present.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Rose and lotus have several carpels that stay free, so their gynoecium is apocarpous.\nStatement II is incorrect. In free central placentation the ovules are borne on a central axis, but septa are absent, as in Dianthus and Primrose.\nA central axis together with septa describes axile placentation, not free central.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-075","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":75,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In an inflorescence where flowers are borne laterally in an acropetal succession, the position of the youngest floral bud in the floral axis shall be","image":"","options":["Proximal","Distal","Intercalary","Any where"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Acropetal succession means flowers develop from the base towards the tip of the floral axis. This is the pattern seen in a racemose inflorescence.\nThe oldest flowers therefore sit at the base (proximal end). The youngest buds are at the apex (distal end).\nThe apex keeps growing and producing new buds, so the youngest bud is always at the tip.\n'Proximal' fails because that is where the oldest flowers lie. 'Intercalary' and 'anywhere' do not fit an ordered acropetal sequence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q2)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-076","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":76,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the rachis represents which part of a leaf?","image":"","options":["Petiole","Midrib","Leaf base","Stipule"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a pinnately compound leaf, the leaflets are borne on a common axis called the rachis.\nNCERT states that the rachis represents the midrib of the leaf.\nIt is not the petiole, which is the stalk below the leaflet-bearing region. It is also not the leaf base or a stipule.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-077","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":77,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the gynoecium is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Each ovary bears one or more ovules attached to a flattened, cushion-like placenta","The style is the enlarged basal part of the carpel on which the ovary lies","Carpels are fused in a syncarpous gynoecium, as in tomato","After fertilisation, the ovules develop into seeds and the ovary matures into a fruit"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Ovules are attached to a flattened, cushion-like placenta inside the ovary.\n(2) Incorrect: The ovary is the enlarged basal part of the carpel. The style is the elongated tube above it that joins the ovary to the stigma. This statement reverses the two.\n(3) Correct: Tomato has a syncarpous gynoecium with fused carpels.\n(4) Correct: After fertilisation, ovules become seeds and the ovary becomes the fruit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-078","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":78,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In lily, the stamens are attached to the perianth. Such stamens are described as:","image":"","options":["Epipetalous","Polyadelphous","Epiphyllous","Monoadelphous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: stamens attached to the perianth are called epiphyllous. Lily is the standard example.\nLily has no separate sepals and petals. Its outer whorls form a perianth, so the stamens attach to perianth members.\nEpipetalous is the tempting wrong option. It means stamens attached to petals, as in brinjal, and lily has no true petals.\nPolyadelphous and monoadelphous describe stamens fused with each other, not attached to other floral parts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-079","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":79,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A drupe develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded.\nStatement II: In coconut, the mesocarp is fleshy and edible, just as in mango.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A drupe develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded. Mango and coconut are both examples.\nStatement II is incorrect. Mango has a fleshy, edible mesocarp, but the coconut mesocarp is fibrous (coir).\nSo Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-080","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":80,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are main functions of the root system as described in NCERT?\n(i) Absorption of water and minerals\n(ii) Anchorage of plant parts\n(iii) Storage of reserve food material\n(iv) Synthesis of plant growth regulators\n(v) Photosynthesis\n(vi) Bearing flowers and fruits","image":"","options":["Three","Four","Five","Six"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Absorption of water and minerals: a main root function.\n(ii) Anchorage of plant parts: a main root function.\n(iii) Storage of reserve food material: a main root function.\n(iv) Synthesis of plant growth regulators: a main root function.\n(v) Photosynthesis: a leaf function, not a root function.\n(vi) Bearing flowers and fruits: a function of the stem or shoot, not the root.\nSo four of the listed items are main root functions.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-081","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":81,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem. In what order are they arranged on the stem?","image":"","options":["Basipetal","Acropetal","Random","Centrifugal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: leaves develop at the nodes from the shoot apical meristem in acropetal order.\nThis means older leaves sit lower on the stem and younger leaves sit nearer the apex.\nBasipetal order is the tempting wrong option. It describes the arrangement of flowers in a cymose inflorescence, not leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-082","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":82,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Solanaceae, the potato (Solanum tuberosum) is an example of a plant whose underground part is a modified root.\nStatement II: In Solanaceae, the gynoecium is bicarpellary with obliquely placed carpels, and the ovary is bilocular with a swollen placenta bearing many ovules.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect: NCERT gives the potato as an underground stem modified into a stem tuber. It is not a root, since it bears nodes and buds (eyes).\nStatement II is correct: the Solanaceae gynoecium is bicarpellary with obliquely placed carpels. The ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen axile placenta bearing many ovules.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-083","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":83,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which option gives the correct order of regions in a root tip, starting from the apex and moving upward towards the stem?","image":"","options":["Root cap → region of elongation → region of meristematic activity → region of maturation","Root cap → region of meristematic activity → region of elongation → region of maturation","Region of meristematic activity → root cap → region of maturation → region of elongation","Root cap → region of maturation → region of elongation → region of meristematic activity"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The root cap covers the apex.\nThe region of meristematic activity lies a few millimetres above the root cap, where new cells are produced.\nThe region of elongation lies just proximal to the meristem, where those new cells enlarge.\nThe region of maturation lies proximal to elongation, where cells differentiate.\nOption (1) is the common trap. Cells must be produced before they can elongate, so elongation cannot come before the meristem.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-084","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":84,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\n(A) Stipules\n(B) Pulvinus\n(C) Midrib\n(D) Petiole\nList-II\n(I) Middle prominent vein of the lamina\n(II) Swollen leaf base in some legumes\n(III) Two small leaf-like lateral structures at the leaf base\n(IV) Helps hold the blade to light\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-I, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Stipules: two small leaf-like lateral structures at the leaf base, so A-III.\n(B) Pulvinus: swollen leaf base seen in some legumes, so B-II.\n(C) Midrib: the middle prominent vein of the lamina, so C-I.\n(D) Petiole: holds the blade towards light, so D-IV.\nOnly option (1) gives this combination.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-085","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":85,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A stamen that does not produce fertile pollen grains is called a:","image":"","options":["Staminode","Pistillode","Pollinium","Connective"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT calls a sterile stamen, one that produces no fertile pollen, a staminode.\nThe other terms do not fit:\n- A pistillode is a sterile pistil or carpel.\n- A pollinium is a mass of pollen grains.\n- A connective is tissue joining the anther lobes.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-086","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":86,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots arising from the base of the stem, forming a fibrous root system.\nStatement II: In the banyan tree, roots arise from parts of the plant other than the radicle and are called adventitious roots.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in monocots such as wheat, the primary root is short-lived. It is replaced by many roots from the stem base, forming a fibrous root system.\nStatement II is correct: roots that arise from parts other than the radicle are adventitious. Examples are the prop roots of banyan, and the roots of Monstera and grass.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-087","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":87,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Root cap\nB. Region of meristematic activity\nC. Region of elongation\nD. Region of maturation\nList-II\nI. Rapid enlargement of cells causing increase in length\nII. Differentiation of cells and formation of root hairs\nIII. Protection of the tender root apex\nIV. Repeated division of small cells with dense protoplasm\nChoose the correct answer:","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Root cap: protects the tender root apex → III.\nB. Region of meristematic activity: repeated division of small cells with dense protoplasm → IV.\nC. Region of elongation: rapid enlargement of cells increases root length → I.\nD. Region of maturation: cells differentiate and some epidermal cells form root hairs → II.\nSo the correct match is A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-088","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":88,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about leaves are correct?\n(i) Veins act as channels for transporting water, minerals and food materials.\n(ii) Stipules are borne at the tip of the lamina.\n(iii) The midrib is the middle prominent vein of the lamina.\n(iv) The bud in the axil of a leaf later develops into a branch.\n(v) Parallel venation is characteristic of most dicotyledons.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: veins transport water, minerals and food.\n(ii) Incorrect: stipules are borne at the leaf base, not at the lamina tip.\n(iii) Correct: the midrib is the middle prominent vein of the lamina.\n(iv) Correct: the axillary bud later grows into a branch.\n(v) Incorrect: parallel venation is typical of monocots, while dicots usually have reticulate venation.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-089","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":89,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the root tip are correct?\n(i) The root cap is present at the apex of the root.\n(ii) The meristematic region lies a few millimetres above the root cap.\n(iii) Cells of the meristematic region have dense protoplasm.\n(iv) Root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.\n(v) Root hairs develop from cortical cells.\n(vi) The region of maturation lies distal (closer to the tip) to the region of elongation.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the root cap covers the root apex.\n(ii) Correct: the meristematic region lies a few mm above the root cap.\n(iii) Correct: meristematic cells have dense protoplasm.\n(iv) Correct: root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil.\n(v) Incorrect: root hairs arise from epidermal cells, not cortical cells.\n(vi) Incorrect: the maturation zone lies proximal to elongation, i.e. farther from the tip.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-090","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":90,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (use) with List-II (Solanaceae plant):\nList-I\n(A) Spice\n(B) Medicine\n(C) Ornamental\n(D) Food\nList-II\n(I) Petunia\n(II) Brinjal\n(III) Chilli\n(IV) Ashwagandha","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Spice: chilli (Capsicum) is used as a spice, so A-III.\nB. Medicine: ashwagandha (Withania) is a medicinal plant, so B-IV.\nC. Ornamental: petunia is grown as an ornamental, so C-I.\nD. Food: brinjal is eaten as a vegetable, so D-II.\nOption (1) is the only one with all four pairs right. Do not put chilli under medicine.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-091","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":91,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\nA. Hypogynous flower\nB. Perigynous flower\nC. Epigynous flower\nD. Zygomorphic flower\nList-II\ni. Pea\nii. Cucumber\niii. Plum\niv. Mustard\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i","A-iii, B-iv, C-ii, D-i","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-ii, B-iii, C-iv, D-i"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A–iv: Mustard is hypogynous, with a superior ovary.\nB–iii: Plum is perigynous, with a half-inferior ovary.\nC–ii: Cucumber is epigynous, with an inferior ovary.\nD–i: Pea is zygomorphic, divisible into similar halves in only one vertical plane.\nThis matches A-iv, B-iii, C-ii, D-i.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-092","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":92,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I (Position of floral parts)\n(A) Hypogynous\n(B) Perigynous\n(C) Epigynous\nList-II (Example)\n(I) Cucumber\n(II) Brinjal\n(III) Peach\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-II, B-III, C-I","A-III, B-II, C-I","A-II, B-I, C-III","A-I, B-III, C-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Hypogynous: the ovary is superior and the other parts arise below it, as in brinjal. So A–II.\n(B) Perigynous: the ovary is half-inferior and the parts arise on the thalamus rim, as in peach. So B–III.\n(C) Epigynous: the ovary is inferior and the thalamus fuses with it, as in cucumber. So C–I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-093","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":93,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the papilionaceous corolla of a pea flower, the largest petal is called the","image":"","options":["Keel","Wing","Standard","Sepal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: Pea has a papilionaceous corolla of five petals arranged in vexillary aestivation.\nThe largest, posterior petal is the standard (vexillum).\nTwo lateral petals are the wings. The two smallest anterior petals form the keel (carina), which encloses the stamens and pistil.\nThe keel is the tempting option because it is a distinctive structure, but it is made of the smallest petals.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-094","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":94,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A region of the root tip has very small, thin-walled cells with dense protoplasm that divide repeatedly. This region is the:","image":"","options":["Region of maturation","Region of elongation","Region of meristematic activity","Root cap"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: A few millimetres above the root cap lies the region of meristematic activity. Its cells are very small and thin-walled, have dense protoplasm, and divide repeatedly.\nThe features described match this region exactly, so (3) fits.\nThe region of elongation is the tempting wrong option. Its cells enlarge and lengthen rapidly rather than staying small and dividing.\nThe root cap only protects the apex, and the maturation zone has differentiating cells.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-095","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":95,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Castor seeds are described as endospermic seeds.\nReason (R): In castor, the endosperm formed as a result of double fertilisation persists in the mature seed as a food-storing tissue.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Castor is a classic NCERT example of an endospermic (albuminous) seed.\nR is true. The endosperm forms after double fertilisation and remains in the mature castor seed as food-storage tissue.\nR is the correct explanation of A, because a seed is called endospermic precisely when its endosperm persists in the mature seed.\nCompare non-endospermic seeds such as gram and pea, where the endosperm is used up before the seed matures.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-096","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":96,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If the root hairs of a seedling are damaged during transplanting, its uptake of water and minerals falls, even though its root cap and meristematic region are intact.\nStatement II: Root hairs are produced by cells of the region of elongation, which then stop dividing.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Root hairs are the main structures that absorb water and minerals, so damaging them reduces uptake even if the root cap and meristem are intact.\nStatement II is incorrect. Root hairs are formed by some epidermal cells in the region of maturation, not in the region of elongation.\nSo Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-097","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":97,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In the maize grain, the single large, shield-shaped cotyledon is called the scutellum.\nStatement II: In the maize embryo, the plumule is enclosed in a sheath called the coleorhiza.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: the single, large, shield-shaped cotyledon of the maize embryo is the scutellum.\nStatement II is incorrect: the plumule is enclosed in the coleoptile. The coleorhiza is the sheath around the radicle.\nTip: coleo-rhiza goes with the root (radicle); coleo-ptile goes with the shoot (plumule).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-098","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":98,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A leaf is a lateral outgrowth of the stem that develops exogenously at a node.\nStatement II: On a shoot, the youngest leaves are found towards the base and the oldest near the apex.\nChoose the correct option:","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A leaf is a lateral, flattened outgrowth that develops exogenously at a node and bears a bud in its axil.\nStatement II is incorrect. Leaves arise in acropetal order, so the youngest leaves lie near the shoot apex and the oldest towards the base. The statement reverses this.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-099","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":99,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower has the following features:\n(i) Its floral appendages are in multiples of 4.\n(ii) It can be cut into two equal halves along any radial plane passing through the centre.\n(iii) The gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus, and the other parts lie below it.\nWhich option correctly describes this flower?","image":"","options":["Tetramerous, zygomorphic, hypogynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary","Pentamerous, actinomorphic, epigynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, perigynous with half-inferior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Floral appendages in multiples of 4 make the flower tetramerous.\n(ii) Division into equal halves along any radial plane means it is actinomorphic.\n(iii) The gynoecium in the highest position, with the other parts below it, marks a hypogynous flower. Its ovary is superior, as in mustard.\nOption (1) is the trap. 'Highest position' means a superior ovary, not an inferior one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-100","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":100,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student examines transverse sections of two ovaries.\nOvary X: several chambers separated by septa, with ovules attached to a placenta in the central axis.\nOvary Y: a single chamber with no septa, with ovules borne on a central column.\nWhich pair of plants could X and Y belong to, respectively?","image":"","options":["Argemone and Dianthus","Lemon and Marigold","Lemon and Primrose","Pea and Primrose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ovary X has several chambers, septa and an axial placenta. This is axile placentation, as in lemon.\nOvary Y has one chamber, no septa and ovules on a central column. This is free central placentation, as in Primrose and Dianthus.\nOption (1) fails because Argemone is parietal. Option (2) fails because marigold is basal. Option (4) fails because pea is marginal.\nSo X and Y are lemon and Primrose.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-101","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":101,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Plants of the family Solanaceae typically produce which type of fruit?","image":"","options":["Drupe or nut","Legume or pod","Berry or capsule","Siliqua or achene"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT describes the fruit of Solanaceae as a berry or a capsule. Tomato and brinjal are berries, and Datura has a capsule.\nLegume (pod) is the fruit of Fabaceae, and siliqua is the fruit of Brassicaceae.\nDrupes such as mango and coconut belong to other families.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-102","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":102,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A transverse section of a floral bud shows the following: there are five petals; the largest posterior petal overlaps two lateral petals, and these two lateral petals in turn overlap the two smallest anterior petals. Which of the following correctly identifies this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Imbricate aestivation; the largest petal is the keel and the smallest pair forms the wings; seen in Cassia","Vexillary aestivation; the largest petal is the standard, the lateral pair are wings and the anterior pair form the keel; seen in pea","Twisted aestivation; the largest petal is the standard and the anterior pair are wings; seen in cotton","Vexillary aestivation; the largest petal is the standard, the lateral pair form the keel and the anterior pair are wings; seen in gulmohur"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Five petals, with the largest posterior petal overlapping two laterals that overlap two anterior petals, is vexillary (papilionaceous) aestivation.\nThe largest petal is the standard (vexillum), the lateral pair are the wings (alae), and the anterior pair form the keel (carina). This is seen in pea and bean.\nOption (4) swaps the wings and keel and wrongly cites gulmohur, which is imbricate.\nCassia (1) is imbricate, and cotton (3) is twisted.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-103","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":103,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about seeds:\n(a) Castor is a dicot whose mature seed retains endosperm.\n(b) Orchids are an exception among monocots as their seeds are non-endospermic.\n(c) In a maize seed, the coleorhiza is the sheath enclosing the plumule.\n(d) The hilum is the scar of attachment of the seed to the fruit, and the micropyle lies above it.\n(e) In gram seeds, reserve food is stored mainly in the endosperm.\nWhich of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["(a), (b) and (d) only","(a), (c) and (d) only","(b), (d) and (e) only","(a), (b), (c) and (e) only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: castor is a dicot with an endospermic seed.\n(b) Correct: monocot seeds are generally endospermic, but orchid seeds are non-endospermic.\n(c) Incorrect: the coleorhiza sheathes the radicle. The coleoptile encloses the plumule.\n(d) Correct: the hilum is the scar where the seed was attached to the fruit, and the micropyle is a small pore just above it.\n(e) Incorrect: gram is non-endospermic. Its food is stored in the fleshy cotyledons.\nSo only (a), (b) and (d) are correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-104","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":104,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The stamens of lily are described as epiphyllous rather than epipetalous.\nReason (R): In lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct from each other and together are termed perianth.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT calls lily stamens epiphyllous, meaning attached to the perianth.\nR is true: in lily, the calyx and corolla are not distinct, and together they form the perianth.\nLily has no separate petals, so its stamens cannot be called epipetalous. They attach to perianth members instead.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-105","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":105,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following have flowers with an inferior ovary?\nGuava, cucumber, ray florets of sunflower, plum, mustard, brinjal, peach","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Epigynous (inferior ovary): guava, cucumber and the ray florets of sunflower. Here the thalamus grows around and fuses with the ovary.\nPerigynous (half-inferior ovary): plum and peach. These are not fully inferior, which is the trap.\nHypogynous (superior ovary): mustard and brinjal.\nSo three have an inferior ovary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-106","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":106,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower has the following features:\n- Its floral appendages occur in multiples of four.\n- It can be cut into two equal halves along any radial plane through its centre.\n- Its gynoecium occupies the highest position on the thalamus, with all other parts below it.\nWhich description fits this flower?","image":"","options":["Trimerous, zygomorphic, epigynous with inferior ovary","Tetramerous, zygomorphic, perigynous with half-inferior ovary","Tetramerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary","Pentamerous, actinomorphic, hypogynous with superior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Appendages in multiples of 4 make the flower tetramerous.\nEqual halves along any radial plane make it actinomorphic.\nA gynoecium at the highest position, with all other parts below it, means hypogynous with a superior ovary, as in mustard.\nOnly option (3) combines tetramerous, actinomorphic and hypogynous.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-107","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":107,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a compound leaf, an axillary bud is present in the axil of each leaflet.\nStatement II: Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem and are arranged in acropetal order.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. In a compound leaf, a bud is present only in the axil of the petiole, not in the axils of the leaflets. This helps tell a compound leaf apart from a twig bearing simple leaves.\nStatement II is correct. Leaves arise from the shoot apical meristem and are arranged in acropetal order, with the youngest leaves nearest the apex.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-108","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":108,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The placenta is attached to the developing seed near the","image":"","options":["Testa","Hilum","Micropyle","Chalaza"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A developing ovule (seed) is attached to the placenta by a stalk called the funicle.\nThe point where the funicle joins the seed becomes the hilum, a scar on the seed coat.\nThe micropyle is a small pore near the hilum. It is not the point of attachment.\nThe chalaza is the basal region of the ovule, and the testa is simply the seed coat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q8)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-109","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":109,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match the following and choose the correct option\nColumn I — Column II\nA. Aleurone layer — i. without fertilization\nB. Parthenocarpic fruit — ii. Nutrition\nC. Ovule — iii. Double fertilization\nD. Endosperm — iv. Seed","image":"","options":["A-i, B-ii, C-iii, D-iv","A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii","A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A. Aleurone layer: the protein-rich outer layer of the endosperm, so it pairs with nutrition (ii).\nB. Parthenocarpic fruit: develops without fertilization (i), as in banana.\nC. Ovule: after fertilization it matures into the seed (iv).\nD. Endosperm: forms when the second male gamete fuses with the polar nuclei during double fertilization (iii).\nSo the matching is A-ii, B-i, C-iv, D-iii.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q10)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-110","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":110,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A leaflet of a compound leaf can be distinguished from a simple leaf by checking for a bud in its axil.\nReason (R): An axillary bud is present in the axil of the petiole of a leaf but is absent in the axil of the leaflets of a compound leaf.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A simple leaf has an axillary bud in the axil of its petiole. A leaflet has no bud in its axil. So checking the axil tells the two apart.\nR is true. NCERT notes that an axillary bud occurs in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves, but never in the axil of a leaflet.\nR states the exact feature that A relies on, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-111","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":111,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\n(a) Valvate\n(b) Twisted\n(c) Imbricate\n(d) Vexillary\nList-II:\n(i) Gulmohur\n(ii) Bean\n(iii) Calotropis\n(iv) Cotton","image":"","options":["a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii","a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i","a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii","a-i, b-iv, c-iii, d-ii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Valvate: Calotropis, whose petals touch at the margins without overlapping. So a-iii.\n(b) Twisted: cotton, where each petal overlaps the next one regularly. So b-iv.\n(c) Imbricate: gulmohur, where the margins overlap in no particular direction. So c-i.\n(d) Vexillary: bean, which has a papilionaceous corolla. So d-ii.\nOnly option (1) gives this combination. Watch out for swapping gulmohur and cotton.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-112","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":112,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have zygomorphic flowers?\nPea, datura, gulmohur, chilli, bean, Cassia, mustard, canna","image":"","options":["3","5","6","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Zygomorphic means the flower divides into similar halves in only one vertical plane.\nZygomorphic: pea, gulmohur, bean and Cassia, which makes 4.\nActinomorphic: datura, chilli and mustard.\nCanna is asymmetric, not zygomorphic. Counting it would wrongly give 5.\nTotal = 4.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-113","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":113,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a cymose inflorescence, the main axis terminates in a flower and is therefore limited in growth.\nStatement II: In a cymose inflorescence, the flowers are borne in acropetal succession.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In a cyme the main axis ends in a flower, so its growth is limited.\nStatement II is incorrect. In a cyme the oldest flower is at the tip, so the flowers develop in basipetal order. Acropetal succession is a feature of racemose inflorescence.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-114","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":114,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which of the following flowers are the calyx and corolla not distinguishable from each other, so that they are together termed the perianth?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Pea","Lily","China rose"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When the calyx and corolla cannot be told apart, the floral whorl is called the perianth.\nNCERT gives lily as the example.\nMustard, pea and china rose all have a distinct green calyx and a coloured corolla, so they do not have a perianth.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-115","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":115,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","image":"","options":["Sepals are generally green, leaf-like and protect the flower in the bud stage","Corolla may be tubular, bell-shaped, funnel-shaped or wheel-shaped","In imbricate aestivation, the margins of sepals or petals overlap in one particular direction throughout the whorl","In valvate aestivation, sepals or petals just touch one another at the margins without overlapping"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Sepals are usually green and leaf-like, and they protect the flower in the bud stage.\n(2) Correct: The corolla may be tubular, bell-shaped, funnel-shaped or wheel-shaped.\n(3) Incorrect: In imbricate aestivation the margins overlap, but not in any particular direction. A regular one-directional overlap describes twisted aestivation.\n(4) Correct: In valvate aestivation the margins just touch without overlapping.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-116","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":116,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A flower cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane passing through its centre. Such a flower is found in:","image":"","options":["Datura","Canna","Cassia","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A flower that cannot be divided into two similar halves by any vertical plane through its centre is asymmetric (irregular).\nNCERT gives canna as the example.\nDatura and chilli are actinomorphic, so any radial plane gives equal halves.\nCassia is zygomorphic, so only one vertical plane gives similar halves. This makes it the tempting wrong choice.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-117","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":117,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show axile placentation?\nChina rose, Tomato, Lemon, Mustard, Pea, Dianthus, Argemone","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Axile placentation means a multilocular ovary with ovules on a central axis.\nChina rose: axile ✓\nTomato: axile ✓\nLemon: axile ✓\nMustard: parietal ✗\nArgemone: parietal ✗\nPea: marginal ✗\nDianthus: free central ✗\nSo 3 plants show axile placentation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-118","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":118,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: A bud is present in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves, and such buds are borne on the stem at nodes.\nStatement II: Checking for the presence of a bud in its axil can help decide whether a leaf-like structure is an individual leaflet or an entire compound leaf.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: a bud is present in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves, at the nodes of the stem.\nStatement II is correct: leaflets of a compound leaf have no bud in their axils. A bud in the axil therefore shows that the structure is a whole leaf, not a leaflet.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-119","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":119,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A hypothetical flower is described by the floral formula Br % ⚥ $P_{(3+3)}\\,A_{3+3}\\,\\overline{G}_{(3)}$. Which description correctly matches this formula?","image":"","options":["Ebracteate, actinomorphic, bisexual; perianth free; six stamens fused; ovary superior, tricarpellary, syncarpous","Bracteate, zygomorphic, unisexual; perianth fused; six stamens free; ovary inferior, tricarpellary, apocarpous","Bracteate, zygomorphic, bisexual; perianth of six fused segments in two whorls; six free stamens in two whorls; ovary inferior, tricarpellary, syncarpous","Bracteate, actinomorphic, bisexual; perianth fused; six stamens epiphyllous; ovary superior, tricarpellary, syncarpous"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Decode the formula symbol by symbol:\n- Br = bracteate; % = zygomorphic; ⚥ = bisexual.\n- $P_{(3+3)}$ = six perianth segments in two whorls, fused (bracket).\n- $A_{3+3}$ = six stamens in two whorls, free (no bracket).\n- $\\overline{G}_{(3)}$ = inferior ovary (line above G), three fused carpels (syncarpous).\nOnly option (3) matches every symbol. Options (1) and (4) wrongly say actinomorphic and superior.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-120","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":120,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Tap root system\n(b) Fibrous root system\n(c) Adventitious roots\n(d) Secondary roots\nList-II\n(i) Many roots arising from the stem base after the primary root dies early\n(ii) First-order laterals borne on the primary root\n(iii) Primary root together with all its branches\n(iv) Roots arising from plant parts other than the radicle\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Tap root system: the primary root together with all its branches, so (iii).\n(b) Fibrous root system: many roots from the stem base after the primary root dies early, so (i).\n(c) Adventitious roots: arise from plant parts other than the radicle, so (iv).\n(d) Secondary roots: first-order laterals borne on the primary root, so (ii).\nSo the matching is a-iii, b-i, c-iv, d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-121","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":121,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which type of placentation does the placenta form a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary, with the ovules borne on it in two rows?","image":"","options":["Axile","Parietal","Marginal","Basal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in marginal placentation the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture, and ovules are borne on it in two rows, as in pea.\nThe most tempting wrong option is parietal. It also bears ovules on the ovary wall, but on the peripheral wall rather than on a single ventral ridge.\nAxile placentation uses a central axis in a multilocular ovary. Basal placentation has a single ovule at the base.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-122","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":122,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In the bud of a bean flower, which petals are overlapped by another petal and also themselves overlap other petals?","image":"","options":["The standard","The two wings","The two keel petals","Both the standard and the keel petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In vexillary aestivation of the bean bud, the large posterior standard overlaps the two lateral wings.\nThe two wings in turn overlap the two anterior keel petals.\nSo the wings are overlapped by the standard and also overlap the keel.\nThe standard (1) overlaps but is never overlapped. The keel petals (3) are overlapped but overlap nothing. So (4) also fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-123","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":123,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the stem as described for flowering plants?","image":"","options":["Conduction of water, minerals and photosynthates","Spreading out branches bearing leaves, flowers and fruits","Absorption of water and minerals from the soil through fine hair-like outgrowths","Vegetative propagation in some plants"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Stem function: it conducts water, minerals and photosynthates.\n(2) Stem function: it spreads out branches that bear leaves, flowers and fruits.\n(3) Not a stem function: root hairs absorb water and minerals from the soil. The stem only conducts them upward.\n(4) Stem function: in some plants the stem helps in vegetative propagation.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-124","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":124,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which pair of plants does the gynoecium have more than one carpel, with the carpels remaining free from each other?","image":"","options":["Mustard and tomato","Lotus and rose","China rose and lemon","Pea and bean"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A gynoecium with more than one carpel, all free from each other, is called apocarpous. Lotus and rose are the NCERT examples.\nMustard, tomato, china rose and lemon all have fused carpels, so they are syncarpous.\nPea and bean have a single carpel (monocarpellary). They do not have 'more than one carpel', so option (4) fails.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-125","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":125,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a perigynous flower, the gynoecium is in the centre and the other floral parts are located on the rim of the thalamus almost at the same level, so the ovary is half inferior.\nStatement II: In an epigynous flower, the margin of the thalamus grows upward and fuses with the ovary, and the other floral parts arise below the ovary.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. In a perigynous flower the gynoecium sits in the centre. The other parts arise on the rim of the thalamus at almost the same level, so the ovary is half inferior (e.g., plum, rose).\nStatement II is incorrect. In epigyny the thalamus margin grows up and fuses with the ovary, but the other floral parts arise above the ovary, not below it.\nThat is why the ovary is called inferior (e.g., cucumber, guava).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-126","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":126,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which one of the following plants bears flowers that can be divided into two similar halves in only one particular vertical plane?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Datura","Pea","Chilli"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A flower that can be cut into two similar halves along only one vertical plane is zygomorphic (bilaterally symmetrical).\nPea has a papilionaceous corolla with a standard, two wings and a keel. It is zygomorphic.\nMustard, datura and chilli have actinomorphic flowers. These can be halved along any radial plane through the centre.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-127","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":127,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is most likely to have leaves with parallel venation?","image":"","options":["Mustard","China rose","Guava","Wheat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: parallel venation, where veins run parallel within the lamina, is characteristic of most monocots.\nWheat is a monocot (a grass), so its leaves show parallel venation.\nMustard, china rose and guava are dicots. Dicots generally have reticulate venation, where veinlets form a network.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-128","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":128,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student lists the following features for Solanum nigrum (family Solanaceae):\nI. Calyx of five united, persistent sepals with valvate aestivation\nII. Five stamens attached to the petals\nIII. Bicarpellary syncarpous ovary that is inferior with parietal placentation\nIV. Leaves alternate, simple, exstipulate with reticulate venation\nWhich of these features are correct?","image":"","options":["I, II and III only","I, II and IV only","II, III and IV only","I and IV only"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"I. Correct: the calyx has five united (gamosepalous), persistent sepals with valvate aestivation.\nII. Correct: the five stamens are epipetalous.\nIII. Incorrect: the bicarpellary syncarpous ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen axile placenta. It is not inferior with parietal placentation.\nIV. Correct: the leaves are alternate, simple and exstipulate, with reticulate venation.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-129","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":129,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A flower is regarded as a modified shoot.\nReason (R): During flower formation, the shoot apical meristem changes to a floral meristem, the internodes do not elongate, and floral appendages are produced laterally at successive nodes instead of leaves.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. A flower is a modified, condensed shoot.\nR is true. The shoot apical meristem becomes a floral meristem. The internodes do not elongate, so the axis stays condensed. Floral appendages arise laterally at successive nodes in place of leaves.\nThese nodes bearing appendages on a shoot-derived axis are exactly what show the shoot nature of the flower. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-130","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":130,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a germinating seed, the stem of a flowering plant develops from which part of the embryo?","image":"","options":["Radicle","Plumule","Cotyledon","Hilum"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: The stem is the ascending part of the axis and develops from the plumule of the embryo of a germinating seed.\nThe radicle is the tempting wrong option. It develops into the primary root, not the stem.\nCotyledons are seed leaves that store or supply food, so they do not form the axis.\nThe hilum is only a scar on the seed coat.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-131","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":131,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In most monocotyledonous plants, the primary root is short-lived and is replaced by many roots that arise from the base of the stem.\nStatement II: In most dicotyledonous plants, the radicle elongates directly to form a primary root, which bears secondary and tertiary roots.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: in most monocots the primary root is short-lived. It is replaced by many roots arising from the base of the stem, forming the fibrous root system (e.g., wheat).\nStatement II is correct: in most dicots the radicle grows directly into the primary root, which bears secondary and tertiary roots. This is the tap root system (e.g., mustard).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-132","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":132,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: An axillary bud is present in the axil of the petiole of both simple and compound leaves.\nStatement II: Each leaflet of a compound leaf bears an axillary bud in its axil.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. A bud is present in the axil of the petiole in both simple and compound leaves.\nStatement II is incorrect. No bud is present in the axil of the individual leaflets of a compound leaf. This is how a compound leaf is told apart from a branch bearing simple leaves.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-133","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":133,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A gardener places a cut piece of grass stem in moist soil, and roots soon develop from its nodes. These roots are best described as:","image":"","options":["Secondary roots, because they are branches of the primary root","Tap roots, because they grow downward into the soil","Tertiary roots, because they arise late in development","Adventitious roots, because they arise from a part other than the radicle"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Key fact: roots that arise from any part of the plant other than the radicle are called adventitious roots. NCERT gives grass as an example.\nWhy (4) fits: here the roots come from stem nodes, not from the radicle.\nWhy the others fail: secondary and tertiary roots are branches of the radicle-derived primary root. A tap root is the primary root itself.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-134","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":134,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is used to extract the blue dye?","image":"","options":["Trifolium","Indigofera","Lupin","Cassia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Indigofera (family Fabaceae) is the source of indigo, a natural blue dye.\nThe other three are also Fabaceae but are not dye sources:\n- Trifolium (clover) and Lupin are used as fodder or ornamentals.\n- Cassia is used medicinally.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q9)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-135","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":135,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of aestivation) with List-II (example).\nList-I:\nA. Valvate\nB. Twisted\nC. Imbricate\nD. Vexillary\nList-II:\nI. Cassia\nII. Calotropis\nIII. Bean\nIV. Lady's finger","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Valvate: the margins of sepals or petals only touch and do not overlap, as in Calotropis → II.\nB. Twisted: each margin overlaps the next one in a regular direction, as in lady's finger → IV.\nC. Imbricate: the margins overlap but in no particular direction, as in Cassia and gulmohur → I.\nD. Vexillary (papilionaceous): a large standard petal covers the two wings, which cover the keel, as in pea and bean → III.\nThe matching is A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-136","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":136,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In monocotyledons, the leaf base commonly expands into a structure that partially or wholly covers the stem. This structure is the:","image":"","options":["Pulvinus","Petiole","Sheath","Midrib"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: the leaf base attaches the leaf to the stem. In monocots it expands into a sheath that partly or wholly wraps the stem.\nThe pulvinus is a tempting choice because it is also a modified leaf base. But it is a swollen leaf base found in some legumes and does not wrap the stem.\nThe petiole is the leaf stalk, and the midrib is the central vein of the lamina.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-137","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":137,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which plant has whorled phyllotaxy, where more than two leaves arise at a single node?","image":"","options":["China rose","Guava","Alstonia","Mustard"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in whorled phyllotaxy, more than two leaves arise at a node and form a whorl. Alstonia is the standard example.\nChina rose and mustard are tempting, but they bear a single leaf per node, which is alternate phyllotaxy.\nGuava bears a pair of opposite leaves per node, which is opposite phyllotaxy.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-138","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":138,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Coconut is classified as a drupe even though its mesocarp is fibrous rather than fleshy.\nReason (R): Drupes are one-seeded fruits that develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: NCERT describes drupes as one-seeded fruits that develop from monocarpellary superior ovaries.\nA is true: coconut is a drupe even though its mesocarp is fibrous, unlike the fleshy edible mesocarp of mango.\nR explains A. A fruit is classed as a drupe by its origin and its single seed, not by the texture of its mesocarp. So coconut qualifies as a drupe despite its fibrous mesocarp.\nBoth A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-139","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":139,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which member of the family Solanaceae is cited in NCERT as a source of a fumigatory?","image":"","options":["Belladonna","Petunia","Ashwagandha","Tobacco"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists the economic uses of Solanaceae as follows:\n- food: tomato, brinjal, potato\n- spice: chilli\n- medicine: belladonna, ashwagandha\n- fumigatory: tobacco\n- ornamental: petunia\nSo tobacco is the fumigatory. Belladonna and ashwagandha are medicinal, and petunia is ornamental.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-140","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":140,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show alternate phyllotaxy?\nChina rose, Mustard, Guava, Sunflower, Calotropis, Alstonia","image":"","options":["Two","Three","Four","Five"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Alternate phyllotaxy means a single leaf arises at each node.\nChina rose: alternate. Mustard: alternate. Sunflower: alternate.\nGuava and Calotropis: opposite. Alstonia: whorled.\nThat gives three plants with alternate phyllotaxy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-141","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":141,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (structure in a maize seed) with List-II (description) and choose the correct option.\nList-I:\n(A) Scutellum\n(B) Aleurone layer\n(C) Coleoptile\n(D) Coleorhiza\nList-II:\n(i) Sheath enclosing the radicle\n(ii) Large, shield-shaped single cotyledon\n(iii) Sheath enclosing the plumule\n(iv) Proteinous layer separating the endosperm from the embryo","image":"","options":["A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(iii), D-(i)","A-(iv), B-(ii), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(ii), B-(iv), C-(i), D-(iii)","A-(iii), B-(iv), C-(ii), D-(i)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Scutellum: the large, shield-shaped single cotyledon of maize, so (ii).\n(B) Aleurone layer: the proteinous layer that separates the endosperm from the embryo, so (iv).\n(C) Coleoptile: the sheath around the plumule (shoot tip), so (iii).\n(D) Coleorhiza: the sheath around the radicle (root tip), so (i).\nOptions (2) and (3) swap the two sheaths, which is the usual mistake. Remember: 'rhiza' means root.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-142","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":142,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following pairs of a flower and the position of its ovary is incorrectly matched?","image":"","options":["Mustard – superior ovary","Plum – half-inferior ovary","Cucumber – inferior ovary","Brinjal – inferior ovary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Mustard is hypogynous, so its ovary is superior. Correctly matched.\nPlum is perigynous, so its ovary is half-inferior. Correctly matched.\nCucumber is epigynous, so its ovary is inferior. Correctly matched.\nBrinjal is hypogynous, so its ovary is superior, not inferior. This pair is incorrectly matched.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-143","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":143,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An axillary bud is present in the axil of each leaflet of a pinnately compound leaf.\nReason (R): In a pinnately compound leaf, the leaflets are borne on a common axis, the rachis, which represents the midrib of the leaf.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"A is false: a compound leaf has a bud only in the axil of its petiole. The leaflets have no axillary buds, and this is how a compound leaf is told apart from a branch bearing leaves.\nR is true: in a pinnately compound leaf (e.g., neem) the leaflets are borne on the rachis. The rachis represents the midrib.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-144","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":144,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Coconut is classified as a drupe.\nReason (R): The mesocarp of the coconut fruit is fibrous.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true. NCERT groups coconut with mango as a drupe, because it develops from a monocarpellary superior ovary and is one-seeded.\nR is true. The coconut mesocarp is fibrous (the coir).\nR does not explain A. A drupe is defined by its origin and its single seed, not by a fibrous mesocarp. Mango is also a drupe, yet its mesocarp is fleshy.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-145","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":145,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following plants is cited by NCERT as an example of a fibrous root system?","image":"","options":["Mustard","Wheat","Pea","Gram"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: in monocots such as wheat, the primary root is short-lived. Many roots arising from the stem base replace it, forming a fibrous root system.\nWheat is a monocot, so this fits option (2).\nMustard is NCERT's example of a tap root system. Pea and gram are dicots, which also have tap roots.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-146","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":146,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard is written as ⊕ K2+2 C4 A2+4 G(2), with the ovary shown as superior. Which of the following interpretations of this formula and its floral diagram is correct?","image":"","options":["The bracket in G(2) shows adhesion of the two carpels with the stamens","The androecium has six stamens in two whorls, and the ovary is superior","The dot drawn at the top of the floral diagram represents the bract","The corolla consists of four united (gamopetalous) petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: a bracket shows cohesion (fusion within a whorl). Adhesion between whorls is shown by a line drawn above the symbols.\n(2) Correct: A2+4 means six stamens in two whorls (tetradynamous). Mustard is hypogynous, so its ovary is superior.\n(3) Wrong: the dot at the top of a floral diagram marks the mother axis, not the bract.\n(4) Wrong: C4 has no bracket, so the four petals are free (polypetalous).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-147","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":147,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The ovary of mustard shows parietal placentation, yet it appears two-chambered.\nReason (R): A false septum forms in the originally one-chambered ovary of mustard.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: In parietal placentation the ovules develop on the inner wall of the ovary. Yet the mustard ovary appears two-chambered.\nR is true: The mustard ovary is originally one-chambered. A false septum later divides it into two chambers.\nR is the correct explanation of A: The false septum is exactly why a parietal ovary looks two-chambered. Argemone shows the same feature.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-148","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":148,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In both lemon and Primrose the ovules are borne on a central axis, but only in lemon is the ovary divided into chambers.\nStatement II: In basal placentation, as in marigold, many ovules are attached to a placenta at the base of the ovary.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: Lemon has axile placentation, with ovules on a central axis in a chambered (multilocular) ovary. Primrose has free central placentation, with ovules on a central axis but no septa.\nStatement II is incorrect: In basal placentation, as in marigold, the placenta at the base of the ovary bears only a single ovule, not many.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-149","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":149,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the floral formula of a Solanaceae flower, a line is drawn above the symbols C and A.\nReason (R): Adhesion between floral whorls is shown by a line drawn above the symbols, and in Solanaceae the stamens are attached to the petals.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the Solanaceae floral formula shows C and A joined by a line drawn above them.\nR is true: adhesion means union of different whorls. Here the stamens are fused to the petals (epipetalous), and this is shown by the line above the symbols.\nR explains A. Brackets would show cohesion, which is fusion within one whorl, not adhesion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-150","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":150,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which part of the pericarp of a mango do we eat?","image":"","options":["Epicarp","Mesocarp","Endocarp","Seed coat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The mango pericarp (fruit wall) has three layers: epicarp, mesocarp and endocarp.\nThe epicarp is the thin outer skin, which we peel off.\nThe mesocarp is the fleshy, juicy middle layer, and this is the part we eat.\nThe endocarp is the hard, stony layer around the seed, so it is not eaten. The seed coat is not part of the pericarp at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-151","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":151,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A pair of leaves arises at each node of a plant, and the two leaves lie opposite each other. Which of the following plants shows this arrangement?","image":"","options":["Sunflower","Guava","China rose","Alstonia"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Opposite phyllotaxy means a pair of leaves arises at each node, opposite each other. NCERT examples are Calotropis and guava.\nSunflower and china rose bear one leaf per node, so they are alternate.\nAlstonia bears more than two leaves per node, so it is whorled.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-152","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":152,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the calyx and corolla of a member of Solanaceae such as Solanum nigrum?","image":"","options":["Calyx polysepalous and twisted; corolla polypetalous and imbricate","Calyx gamosepalous, persistent and valvate; corolla gamopetalous and valvate","Calyx gamosepalous and imbricate; corolla gamopetalous and twisted","Calyx polysepalous and valvate; corolla gamopetalous and vexillary"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Solanaceae (e.g. Solanum nigrum) has a calyx of five united sepals. It is gamosepalous, persistent and valvate.\nThe corolla has five united petals, so it is gamopetalous, and it is also valvate.\nTwisted aestivation is the china rose type and vexillary is the pea type, so (3) and (4) fail.\nThe sepals are not free, so (1) and (4) fail.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-153","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":153,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (type of placentation) with List-II (example):\nList-I\nA. Axile\nB. Parietal\nC. Free central\nD. Basal\nList-II\nI. Argemone\nII. Marigold\nIII. Lemon\nIV. Primrose","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A. Axile: ovules sit on a central axis in a multilocular ovary, as in lemon → III.\nB. Parietal: ovules sit on the inner wall of a one-chambered ovary, as in Argemone → I.\nC. Free central: ovules sit on a central axis with no septa, as in Primrose → IV.\nD. Basal: a single ovule sits at the base of the ovary, as in marigold → II.\nSo the matching is A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II. Do not swap free central (Primrose) with axile (lemon).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-154","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":154,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In members of Solanaceae, the stamens are epipetalous and the ovary is superior and bilocular with axile placentation.\nStatement II: Coconut, like mango, is a drupe in which the mesocarp is fleshy and edible.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I: Correct. In Solanaceae (e.g., Solanum, Petunia), the five stamens are epipetalous. The ovary is bicarpellary, superior and bilocular, with axile placentation on a swollen placenta bearing many ovules.\nStatement II: Incorrect. Coconut is a drupe, like mango, but its mesocarp is fibrous. The edible part of coconut is the endosperm.\nMango is the drupe whose mesocarp is fleshy and edible.\nSo Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-155","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":155,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A reduced leaf is present at the base of the pedicel of a flower. Such a flower is termed:","image":"","options":["Ebracteate","Perigynous","Bracteate","Unisexual"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A bract is a reduced leaf found at the base of the pedicel.\nFlowers that bear a bract are called bracteate, so this option fits.\nEbracteate is the tempting opposite term. It means the flower lacks bracts.\nPerigynous describes the position of the ovary, and unisexual describes the sex of the flower. Neither refers to bracts.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-156","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":156,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants show twisted aestivation in their corolla?\nChina rose, Cassia, Cotton, Calotropis, Lady's finger, Gulmohur, Pea","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Twisted aestivation means one margin of each petal overlaps the next in a regular direction.\nPlants with twisted aestivation: china rose, cotton, lady's finger, which makes 3.\nPlants that do not fit:\n- Cassia and gulmohur are imbricate.\n- Calotropis is valvate.\n- Pea is vexillary.\nSo the count is 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-157","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":157,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(a) Aleurone layer\n(b) Scutellum\n(c) Coleoptile\n(d) Tegmen\nList-II\n(i) Sheath enclosing the plumule\n(ii) Inner layer of the seed coat\n(iii) Proteinous layer separating the endosperm from the embryo\n(iv) Large, shield-shaped single cotyledon\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Aleurone layer: the proteinous layer that covers the endosperm and separates it from the embryo, so (iii).\n(b) Scutellum: the large, shield-shaped single cotyledon of maize, so (iv).\n(c) Coleoptile: the sheath enclosing the plumule, so (i).\n(d) Tegmen: the inner layer of the seed coat, so (ii). The outer layer is the testa.\nThe correct matching is a-iii, b-iv, c-i, d-ii.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-158","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":158,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In members of Solanaceae, the calyx is gamosepalous and shows valvate aestivation.\nStatement II: Aestivation is the arrangement of sepals or petals in a floral bud with respect to the members of the other floral whorls.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Solanaceae has five united (gamosepalous) sepals with valvate aestivation, and the calyx is persistent.\nStatement II is incorrect. Aestivation is the arrangement of sepals or petals in a floral bud with respect to the other members of the same whorl, not other whorls.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-159","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":159,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Roots that arise from any part of the plant other than the radicle are called:","image":"","options":["Tap roots","Adventitious roots","Secondary roots","Tertiary roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT fact: roots that arise from any plant part other than the radicle are called adventitious roots. Examples are grass, Monstera and the banyan tree.\nThis fits option (2).\nTap, secondary and tertiary roots all come from the radicle, either as the primary root or as its branches, so they do not fit.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-160","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":160,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In Salvia and mustard, the filaments of the stamens within the same flower vary in length.\nStatement II: In brinjal, the stamens are united into more than two bundles.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. NCERT cites Salvia and mustard as examples of filaments of different lengths within the same flower.\nStatement II is incorrect. Brinjal is the example of epipetalous stamens, which are attached to the petals.\nStamens united into more than two bundles (polyadelphous) are seen in citrus, not brinjal.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-161","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":161,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the main functions of the root system?","image":"","options":["Absorption of water and minerals from the soil","Providing anchorage to the plant parts","Synthesis of plant growth regulators","Photosynthesis to produce food for the plant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"NCERT lists four main root functions: absorption of water and minerals, anchorage, storage of reserve food, and synthesis of plant growth regulators.\nOptions (1), (2) and (3) all appear in that list.\nPhotosynthesis is mainly carried out by green leaves, not by roots. So it is not a main root function.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-162","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":162,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants have stamens united among themselves into one or more bundles?\nChina rose, pea, citrus, brinjal, lily, mustard","image":"","options":["Two","Four","Five","Three"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Fused among themselves:\n(i) China rose has one bundle (monoadelphous).\n(ii) Pea has two bundles (diadelphous).\n(iii) Citrus has many bundles (polyadelphous).\nNot fused among themselves: brinjal (epipetalous) and lily (epiphyllous) have stamens attached to other floral parts. Mustard has free stamens that only differ in filament length.\nTotal = 3.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-163","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":163,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the maize grain is incorrect?","image":"","options":["The seed coat is membranous and generally fused with the fruit wall.","The embryo lies in a groove at one end of the bulky endosperm.","The embryo has two cotyledons, one of which is enlarged into the scutellum.","A proteinous aleurone layer forms the outer covering of the endosperm and separates it from the embryo."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct. The maize seed coat is membranous and fused with the fruit wall.\n(2) Correct. The small embryo lies in a groove at one end of the bulky endosperm.\n(3) Incorrect. Maize is a monocot, so its embryo has only ONE cotyledon, the shield-shaped scutellum.\n(4) Correct. The proteinous aleurone layer covers the endosperm and separates it from the embryo.\nThe incorrect statement is (3).\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-164","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":164,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A student marks the terminal 1 cm of a young root with ink dots at equal intervals of 1 mm, starting from the tip. After 24 hours of growth, the dots in one particular part of the root have moved farthest apart. These widely spaced dots most likely lie in:","image":"","options":["The root cap, because it is pushed through the soil","The region of maturation, because root hairs form there","The region of elongation, just behind the meristematic zone","The region of meristematic activity, because its cells divide repeatedly"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Ink dots separate wherever cells lengthen the most.\nCells of the region of elongation undergo rapid enlargement, so the root lengthens mainly there. Dots in this zone end up farthest apart.\nOption (4) is the tempting wrong choice. Meristematic cells divide repeatedly but stay small, so the spacing there changes little.\nMaturation-zone cells have stopped elongating and are differentiating. The root cap does not elongate.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-165","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":165,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"A twig has exactly two leaf-like structures at each node, lying opposite each other. Each structure has an axillary bud only at the base of its stalk. Its several blades are arranged along a common central axis, and no buds occur at the bases of these blades. Which description is correct?","image":"","options":["Simple leaves in whorled phyllotaxy","Palmately compound leaves in alternate phyllotaxy","Pinnately compound leaves in opposite phyllotaxy","Each blade is a simple leaf, and the central axis is a branch"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The blades have no axillary buds, so they are leaflets of one compound leaf, not separate simple leaves.\nThe axis bearing the leaflets is the rachis, and leaflets along a rachis make a pinnately compound leaf. It is not palmate, where leaflets arise from one point at the petiole tip.\nThe only buds sit at the base of each whole stalk, so there are two leaves per node, opposite each other. This is opposite phyllotaxy.\nOption (4) fails because a branch would bear buds in the axils of its leaves.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-166","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":166,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Stamens are united into a single bundle in china rose but into more than two bundles in citrus.\nStatement II: Stamens attached to the petals, as in brinjal, are described as epiphyllous.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. China rose has all its stamens united into one bundle (monoadelphous). Citrus has stamens united into more than two bundles (polyadelphous).\nStatement II is incorrect. Stamens attached to petals, as in brinjal, are called epipetalous. The term epiphyllous means stamens attached to the perianth, as in lily.\nSo Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-167","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":167,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the gynoecium is incorrect?","image":"","options":["In Solanaceae, the ovary is superior and bilocular, with a swollen placenta bearing many ovules in axile placentation","Mustard has a syncarpous gynoecium with parietal placentation","In sunflower, the placenta develops at the base of the ovary and bears many ovules","In pea, the ovules form two rows on a ridge along the ventral suture"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: Solanaceae has a superior, bilocular ovary with a swollen placenta bearing many ovules in axile placentation.\n(2) Correct: mustard has a syncarpous gynoecium with parietal placentation.\n(3) Incorrect: sunflower has basal placentation, where the basal placenta bears only a single ovule. 'Many ovules' is wrong.\n(4) Correct: pea has marginal placentation, with two rows of ovules on a ventral ridge.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-168","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":168,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Roots developed from parts of the plant other than radicle are called","image":"","options":["Taproots","Fibrous roots","Adventitious roots","Nodular roots"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In dicots, the primary root grows from the radicle and forms the taproot system.\nRoots arising from any other part of the plant, such as stem nodes or leaves, are called adventitious roots. Examples are grass, Monstera and the prop roots of banyan.\nFibrous roots in monocots also replace the radicle-derived root. However, the term defined by origin from non-radicle parts is 'adventitious'.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"Source: NCERT Exemplar Biology Class 11, Ch 5 (MCQ Q4)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-169","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":169,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In which type of placentation are the ovules borne in two rows on a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary, as seen in pea?","image":"","options":["Axile","Marginal","Parietal","Free central"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In marginal placentation the placenta forms a ridge along the ventral suture of the ovary.\nOvules are borne on this ridge in two rows. Pea is the standard example.\nAxile placentation (china rose, tomato) has ovules on a central axis in a multilocular ovary.\nParietal placentation (mustard) has ovules on the inner wall of the ovary.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-170","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":170,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard (Brassicaceae) is written as $\\oplus$ $K_{2+2}$ $C_4$ $A_{2+4}$ $\\underline{G}_{(2)}$. Which inference drawn from this formula is correct?","image":"","options":["The four sepals are fused, arranged in two whorls of two each","There are six free stamens arranged in two whorls, an outer of two and an inner of four","The gynoecium consists of two free carpels with a superior ovary","The flower is zygomorphic with four petals"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a floral formula, brackets show fusion of parts.\n$A_{2+4}$ has no brackets, so there are six free stamens in two whorls: an outer whorl of 2 and an inner whorl of 4 (tetradynamous). Option (2) is correct.\n$K_{2+2}$ also has no brackets, so the four sepals are free, not fused. Option (1) is wrong.\n$\\underline{G}_{(2)}$ shows two fused carpels (syncarpous) with a superior ovary, so they are not free. Option (3) is wrong.\n$\\oplus$ means the flower is actinomorphic, not zygomorphic. Option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-171","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":171,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In silk cotton, all the leaflets of a leaf are attached at a common point at the tip of the petiole. Such a leaf is described as:","image":"","options":["Pinnately compound","Simple with deep incisions","Palmately compound","Simple with reticulate venation"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"In a palmately compound leaf, all the leaflets are attached at a common point, the tip of the petiole. Silk cotton is the NCERT example.\nA pinnately compound leaf (e.g., neem) instead bears its leaflets along a rachis.\nThe leaf cannot be simple, because the lamina is divided into separate leaflets.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-172","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":172,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as that of neem, the leaflets are borne on a common axis. This axis, called the rachis, represents which part of the leaf?","image":"","options":["The petiole","The midrib","The leaf base","The axillary bud"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In a pinnately compound leaf such as neem, the leaflets are borne on a common axis called the rachis.\nNCERT states that the rachis represents the midrib of the leaf.\nThe petiole is the tempting wrong answer, but it is the stalk below the rachis and bears no leaflets.\nThe leaf base attaches the leaf to the stem, and the axillary bud lies in the axil of the petiole. Neither carries the leaflets.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-173","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":173,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Leaves on a stem are arranged in an acropetal order.\nReason (R): Leaves originate from the shoot apical meristem.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"R is true: leaves arise from the shoot apical meristem.\nA is true: leaves are arranged in acropetal order, with younger leaves towards the apex and older ones lower down.\nThe meristem keeps producing new leaves at the apex. This is why the youngest leaves end up at the top, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-174","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":174,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"The floral formula of mustard is $\\oplus$ ⚥ $K_{2+2}\\,C_4\\,A_{2+4}\\,\\underline{G}_{(2)}$. How many of the following inferences drawn from it are correct?\n(i) The flower is actinomorphic\n(ii) Sepals are arranged in two whorls of two each\n(iii) There are six stamens in two whorls\n(iv) The two carpels are fused\n(v) The petals are united\n(vi) The ovary is inferior","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\oplus$ means the flower is actinomorphic.\n(ii) Correct: $K_{2+2}$ means two whorls of two sepals each.\n(iii) Correct: $A_{2+4}$ means six stamens in two whorls (tetradynamous).\n(iv) Correct: $G_{(2)}$ in brackets means the two carpels are fused (syncarpous).\n(v) Wrong: $C_4$ has no bracket, so the petals are free.\n(vi) Wrong: the line under G shows a superior ovary.\nSo 4 inferences are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-175","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":175,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following plants bear actinomorphic flowers?\nMustard, Datura, Pea, Chilli, Cassia, Canna, Gulmohur","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Actinomorphic (radially symmetrical) flowers can be divided into two equal halves through any radial plane. Mustard, Datura and chilli have such flowers, which makes 3.\nZygomorphic flowers can be divided into two similar halves along only one vertical plane. Pea, Cassia and gulmohur have zygomorphic flowers.\nCanna has asymmetric flowers, which cannot be divided into two equal halves by any vertical plane.\nThe count of actinomorphic flowers is therefore 3.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-176","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":176,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In a mango fruit, the part that is commonly eaten is the:","image":"","options":["Thin outer epicarp","Stony hard endocarp","Fleshy mesocarp","Seed coat"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"The mango is a drupe. Its pericarp is divided into three layers: a thin outer epicarp, a fleshy middle mesocarp and a stony inner endocarp.\nThe fleshy mesocarp is the part we commonly eat.\nThe endocarp is hard and protects the seed, so it is not eaten.\nDo not mix this up with coconut, where the mesocarp is fibrous.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-177","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":177,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"In some leguminous plants the leaf base becomes swollen. What is this swollen leaf base called?","image":"","options":["Stipule","Pulvinus","Leaf sheath","Rachis"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact (NCERT): in some leguminous plants the leaf base may become swollen. This swollen base is called the pulvinus.\nStipules are small lateral appendages at the leaf base, so they are the tempting wrong option.\nA leaf sheath is the expanded leaf base that clasps the stem in monocots. The rachis is the axis of a pinnately compound leaf.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT11-BIO-MORPHOLOGY-178","exam":"NEET","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Morphology of Flowering Plants","qno":178,"subject":"Biology","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Morphology of Flowering Plants","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The region of elongation lies proximal to the region of meristematic activity in a root tip.\nReason (R): Cells of the region of meristematic activity are large, with thick walls and sparse protoplasm.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. The region of elongation lies just proximal to (above) the region of meristematic activity.\nR is false. Meristematic cells are very small and thin-walled, with dense protoplasm. They are not large, thick-walled or sparse in protoplasm.\nSo A is true but R is false.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 11 Biology, Ch 5 · Morphology of Flowering Plants","difficulty":"medium"}]});
